\section{How the Objects Cooperate in the Game Loop}
\begin{lstlisting}
while True:
    gl.check_events(settings, screen, stats, hud, play_button, hero,
                    monster, bullets, shoot_sound)

    if stats.state == "playing":
        hero.update()
        gl.update_bullets(settings, stats, hud, hero, monster, bullets)
        if stats.state == "playing":
            gl.update_monster(settings, stats, hero, monster, bullets)

    # Scrolling background (two copies stacked vertically).
    y += 5
    if y >= settings.screen_height:
        y -= settings.screen_height
    screen.blit(background, (0, y))
    screen.blit(background, (0, y - settings.screen_height))

    gl.update_screen(settings, screen, stats, hud, hero, monster,
                     bullets, play_button)
    clock.tick(60)
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \code{gl.check\_events(...)} handles key presses (movement, \code{SPACE} to fire, \code{P} to play, \code{Q} to quit), mouse clicks on the Play button, and window closing.
  \item \code{hero.update()} updates the hero's position.
  \item \code{gl.update\_bullets(...)} moves the bullets, removes those that leave the screen, and calls \code{win\_game()} when a bullet hits the monster.
  \item \code{gl.update\_monster(...)} updates the monster and checks whether it touched the hero (\code{take\_damage()} then possibly \code{lose\_game()}).
  \item \code{gl.update\_screen(...)} draws the bullets, hero, monster, HUD and, when the game is not running, the Play button.
  \item \code{clock.tick(60)} limits the loop to about 60 frames per second.
\end{itemize}

The main loop only coordinates the work; it does not contain the details of hero movement, monster behavior or HUD drawing. Those details are spread across the specialized classes and functions.

\subsection*{Game states}
The \code{GameStats.state} attribute drives the flow of the game:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \code{"menu"}: the start screen with the Play button.
  \item \code{"playing"}: a round is running.
  \item \code{"won"}: a bullet hit the monster; the reward is \code{win\_reward + ammo * ammo\_bonus}.
  \item \code{"lost"}: the hero's health reached zero.
\end{itemize}

